ISSUE: cli/cli#14521: `gh repo set-default --unset` should not require a repository argument

### Describe the bug

I noticed that running `gh repo set-default --unset` from a script (or with redirected input) fails unexpectedly, citing a missing repository argument:

```console
$ gh repo set-default --unset </dev/null
repository required when not running interactively

Usage:  gh repo set-default [<repository>] [flags]
```

Since `--unset` is supposed to remove whatever repository is currently set as the default, it doesn't make sense for the command to require it as an argument. In fact, passing an argument is actually accepted, but silently ignored (e.g. if the current default is `upstream`, running `gh repo set-default --unset origin` doesn't fail, but unsets `upstream` as the default and completely ignores `origin`).

A similar pattern was already identified for `gh repo set-default --view`, in #7442, which PR #7441 fixed by [allowing](https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/9b031151a825bda919203c5202876a725d637368/pkg/cmd/repo/setdefault/setdefault.go#L108-L110) `--view` without a repository argument. The same exemption should also apply to `--unset`.

I'd be happy to submit this fix as a PR if the direction sounds right.

### Affected version

gh version 2.101.0 (2026-09-15)
https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/tag/v2.101.0

